# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or alongside source files with .test.js/.spec.js suffix
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel for transpilation
- Separate unit, integration, and snapshot tests into distinct directories

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices and ESLint rules
- Use meaningful test descriptions that read as plain English sentences
- Keep test files focused on a single module or feature
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions with descriptive names for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach for isolated setup and teardown
- Use beforeAll/afterAll only for expensive shared resources (e.g., database connections)

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern in every test
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Keep tests deterministic: avoid Date.now(), Math.random(), or external network calls
- Use expect.assertions(n) for async tests to catch silent failures

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for automatic module mocking
- Use jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without replacing the entire module
- Use jest.fn() for standalone mock functions
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() for setTimeout, setInterval, and Date-dependent logic
- Always call jest.clearAllMocks() or jest.resetAllMocks() in afterEach to prevent state leakage
- Prefer manual mocks in __mocks__ directories for complex or frequently reused mocks

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality checks over toBe for object and array comparisons
- Use toMatchObject for partial object matching
- Use toThrow / toThrowError to assert on thrown exceptions
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions

## Snapshot Testing
- Use toMatchSnapshot() only for stable, intentional UI or serialized output
- Commit snapshots to version control and review them in code reviews
- Prefer toMatchInlineSnapshot() for small outputs to keep context in the test file
- Update snapshots deliberately with --updateSnapshot flag, never blindly

## Async Testing
- Use async/await for all asynchronous test logic
- Return promises or use done callback only when async/await is not feasible
- Use expect.resolves and expect.rejects for promise-based assertions
- Mock network requests with jest-fetch-mock or msw (Mock Service Worker)

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with the built-in v8 or babel-jest provider
- Set meaningful thresholds in jest.config.js (statements, branches, functions, lines)
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns to exclude generated, vendor, or non-testable code
- Generate lcov and HTML reports for local visualization and CI upload

## Performance
- Use --runInBand for debugging but default to parallel execution in CI
- Use testPathPattern or testNamePattern to run targeted test subsets during development
- Cache Jest's transform output with the built-in cache (--cache flag enabled by default)
- Limit the use of global setup/teardown to avoid slowing the entire suite

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions for generating reusable, consistent test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants
- Generate dynamic data with @faker-js/faker when realistic data is needed

## Configuration
- Use jest.config.js (or jest.config.ts) for all configuration; avoid embedding config in package.json
- Define moduleNameMapper for path aliases matching tsconfig.json paths
- Use projects array to manage multi-environment setups (e.g., jsdom for UI, node for API)
- Configure setupFilesAfterFramework for global test utilities like @testing-library/jest-dom

## CI/CD Integration
- Use --ci flag in CI pipelines to fail on new snapshots and prevent interactive prompts
- Output JUnit XML reports with jest-junit for CI pipeline compatibility
- Upload coverage reports to Codecov or SonarQube via CI steps
- Run tests with --forceExit to prevent hanging processes in CI

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths explicitly
- Assert on specific error messages and error types, not just that an error was thrown
- Use expect().rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions
- Test boundary conditions and edge cases alongside the happy path

## Documentation
- Write test descriptions that serve as living documentation for the module's behavior
- Document non-obvious mock setups with a brief inline comment explaining why
- Maintain a jest.config.js with comments for any non-default configuration choices

Remember to leverage Jest's rich ecosystem (jest-extended, @testing-library, msw) and its built-in watch mode for fast, reliable unit testing.
